WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.301
-Our next guest
is a fantastic comedian

00:00:02.301 --> 00:00:04.236
who used to write here
at "Late Night."

00:00:04.236 --> 00:00:05.638
Her book, "Unreliable Narrator:

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:07.507
Me, Myself, and Impostor
Syndrome"

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.542
is on sale now.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:11.678
Aparna Nancherla, everybody.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:12.879
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:25.891 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.891 --> 00:00:27.460
Hi, Aparna.
-Hey!

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.961
-I'm so happy
to have you back here.

00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:33.232
You wrote on the show, 2015.
-Allegedly.

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:35.201
-Allegedly.
-Allegedly. Yes.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.237
-You did have an A-plus joke
that you remember fondly.

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:40.407
-I do.
It was a visual joke,

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:42.174
which means, you know,
the punch line

00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:44.977
is a reveal of a picture.
And you know me.

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:46.779
I love pictures.
-You do love pictures.

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:48.982
That's what we always
said about you.

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:50.216
-But yes.
-We dug it up

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:51.751
and we would love to show it.

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.519
-Okay, great.
-Because I will say this.

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:55.754
It's one of those great jokes,
it ages very well.

00:00:55.754 --> 00:00:57.924
-Okay, great.
-I should let our audience

00:00:57.924 --> 00:00:59.592
know this is pre-pandemic
if you're wondering

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.461
why I'm dressed like
an actual talk show host.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:02.461
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.461 --> 00:01:04.563
Here's Aparna's wonderful joke.

00:01:04.563 --> 00:01:07.600
Pope Francis began his tour
of the United States today,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.802
and he kicked it off
in style with a stop

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:12.405
at his favorite restaurant,
Pope Yes.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:18.244
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:21.480
-I -- Okay.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.116
I remember because, you know,
we run the jokes ahead

00:01:24.116 --> 00:01:27.787
in the meeting during the day
and the look in your eyes

00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:31.123
when you saw the sign
Popeyes and you were like,

00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:33.493
"Oh, Pope Yes,"
and I was like, "Yeah!"

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:34.794
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:37.062
I was so excited because,
you know, monologue writer,

00:01:37.062 --> 00:01:38.464
you write jokes every day.

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:41.634
And, like, the thing about me
that I quickly learned,

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:45.538
I only have about
one or two good ideas a week.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:48.474
and then the rest of the week
I celebrate those ideas.

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:51.744
So, you know, I was really
struggling to come up.

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.046
And that Pope Yes, I was like,

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:55.248
"I didn't believe
in God before, but..."

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:56.548
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.548 --> 00:01:59.052
-Pope Yes.
-Pope Yes. Pope Yes.

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:01.320
-Now, when you were on the show,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.022
did you ever think
you would come back

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.423
and be a guest on the show?

00:02:04.423 --> 00:02:07.160
-No, not under
positive circumstances.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.929
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:11.530
-You thought we would drag you
back in here

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.432
for an investigation?

00:02:13.432 --> 00:02:16.769
-Well, it was like, you know,
"she got canceled inevitably.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.604
Let's find out more."

00:02:18.604 --> 00:02:20.406
-Oh, yeah.
You would be canceled publicly

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:22.308
and we'd bring you back sort of
for a public hearing.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:26.079
-Yes. Yes. It would be like my
first apology to the public.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:27.746
-I got you.
Well, I'm certainly glad

00:02:27.746 --> 00:02:29.415
we're not using
your time here for that

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:32.185
because you have written
a fantastic book.

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:34.720
-Thank you.
-And it is about --

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.789
it is about anxiety
and depression

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:39.124
and impostor syndrome
and self-doubt.

00:02:39.124 --> 00:02:40.926
-Yes.
-Now, even people,

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:43.696
competent people I know
when they write books,

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:46.366
that is when, if they've
never had self-doubt,

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:49.168
that's when it creeps up.
So how hard was it for you

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:51.070
to have that very real
feeling of self-doubt

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:55.674
to then write a book which
brings on self-doubt so often?

00:02:55.674 --> 00:02:57.977
-Yeah. I mean, I decided
to write the book

00:02:57.977 --> 00:03:00.079
because I was struggling
with self-doubt

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:01.881
so acutely
in my actual life.

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:05.517
Like, I had sort of had some
moderate success as a comedian.

00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:07.987
I realized the more
opportunities I got,

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:12.091
the worse my anxiety and, like,
impostor syndrome got.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:13.893
So I was like,
you know, self-doubt.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:16.262
If you have so much to say,
you do some work,

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.331
write me -- write me a book.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:21.734
But the thing
with self-doubt,

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:24.470
it's not a good collaborator.
-Right.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.738
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:27.440
-Like, as soon
as I started writing,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.108
it was just like,
"Who are you to write a book?"

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.910
You know, it's just like...
[ Laughs ]

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.179
Like, it didn't like --

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:36.248
I tried -- In writing the book,
I tried to be like, okay,

00:03:36.248 --> 00:03:37.850
I'm going to learn
to work with it.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.184
But, like,
even before I came out

00:03:39.184 --> 00:03:40.820
for this interview,
I was just like, you know,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.154
trying to think
of positive things.

00:03:42.154 --> 00:03:43.723
I was like, "Be yourself."

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:45.925
And then my self-doubt
was just like,

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:46.926
"Who are you?"

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:48.528
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:50.596
-It's not helpful.
-Not helpful.

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:52.731
-A thing you did, which I
imagine --

00:03:52.731 --> 00:03:54.600
I don't know if it was
because you wanted to do it

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.303
for the readers or you wanted to
do it for yourself,

00:03:57.303 --> 00:03:59.305
but you give
your depression a name.

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:00.773
-Yes.
-And that was I guess

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:03.075
you didn't want to have
to keep writing "depression."

00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:04.910
-Yeah, because, you know,

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.280
depression doesn't have
a great reputation.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.681
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:10.415
I feel like it's earned
its bad reputation.

00:04:10.415 --> 00:04:11.617
-Yes, yes, yes.

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:13.519
But I wanted to put
some more levity in it,

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.421
so I just started
calling it Brenda.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:18.758
-Yeah.
-Because I felt like that was

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:20.893
the perfect amount
of, like, casual,

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:24.096
but also you sort of are like,
"Oh, Brenda's here again."

00:04:24.096 --> 00:04:27.633
You know, like,
you're not so mad,

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:30.402
but you're not psyched
she's there either.

00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:33.005
She's kind of one of those
friends where you're like,

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:35.174
"Do we really like each other?

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.043
I don't know.
She's always here."

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:39.345
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:42.381
-The book has
very funny moments.

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:44.616
There's this sort of levity,
I expect,

00:04:44.616 --> 00:04:46.686
knowing you as a writer
and a stand-up,

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:49.188
but how is writing a book like
this different from stand-up?

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:50.956
Did you find it
completely different?

00:04:50.956 --> 00:04:52.925
-Yeah, I mean,
I felt like -- I feel like

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:54.093
with writing stand-up,

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:55.994
you know, you get your setup,
your punch line,

00:04:55.994 --> 00:04:58.196
you have kind of a clean out
at the end.

00:04:58.196 --> 00:05:01.434
And I feel with a lot of these
topics like mental health

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:03.903
or body image
or like productivity,

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:07.507
like, there's such messy
ongoing internal topics for me

00:05:07.507 --> 00:05:09.842
that I kind of didn't
always want to give myself

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:11.410
that out of a punch line

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.348
and just kind of live
in the mess a little bit more.

00:05:15.348 --> 00:05:17.750
But now I'm noticing
in promoting the book,

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.151
it's a little weird
because it's like

00:05:19.151 --> 00:05:21.086
instead of solving
my self-doubt,

00:05:21.086 --> 00:05:22.454
I just felt like it
ended the book

00:05:22.454 --> 00:05:24.991
just like covered
in my own crap.

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:27.693
And then I'm just like, "Look at
me, I'm covered in crap.

00:05:27.693 --> 00:05:29.829
Don't you want to know why?
Read my book."

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:32.932
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:35.368
-You've talked about
being on stage,

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:37.336
and obviously a lot
of stand-up comedians

00:05:37.336 --> 00:05:39.805
when they're trying
new material will bomb.

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:41.107
-Yes.
-That is an important part

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:43.075
of the process.
-Yes.

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:46.211
-But you would
sort of preplan for bombing.

00:05:46.211 --> 00:05:47.846
-[ Laughs ] Yes. Yes.

00:05:47.846 --> 00:05:50.316
-You would sort of just
have things ready to go in case.

00:05:50.316 --> 00:05:51.951
-Yeah, I was like,
I got to have lines

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:53.386
for when it doesn't go well

00:05:53.386 --> 00:05:56.288
because that is going to be
part of my journey, you know?

00:05:56.288 --> 00:05:58.958
So, like sometimes
if no one's saying anything,

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:01.660
I'd be like,
"Oh, you're laughing so hard,

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:03.162
no sound is coming out.

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:05.297
It's one of those laughs,
you know?"

00:06:05.297 --> 00:06:08.501
Or "Oh, this is something we can
both journal about later,"

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:12.738
you know, like, just because,
you know, when you're bombing,

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:14.073
it's like you're uncomfortable.

00:06:14.073 --> 00:06:17.042
But the audience is also
uncomfortable watching you fail.

00:06:17.042 --> 00:06:18.477
Like, it's not fun
for them either.

00:06:18.477 --> 00:06:19.445
-No.
-Yeah.

00:06:19.445 --> 00:06:20.913
-That is the thing.
-Yeah.

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:22.515
-When you're like,
when people are like,

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:23.683
"That was a bad audience,"
I'm like,

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:27.019
"They were also having
a terrible time."

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:29.722
-And they paid to have
a terrible time,

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:33.525
so they lost money
and had a bad time.

00:06:33.525 --> 00:06:34.960
-I was a guest.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.329
You did a podcast for Audible

00:06:37.329 --> 00:06:39.064
called "Introverts:
A Survival Guide."

00:06:39.064 --> 00:06:40.866
-Yes.
-And I really enjoyed

00:06:40.866 --> 00:06:42.334
talking to you.

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:44.136
Did you enjoy
doing that podcast?

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:46.305
Do you feel like --
Were you setting out

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:48.006
to make a podcast
for people

00:06:48.006 --> 00:06:51.777
that were facing the same
difficulties you were having?

00:06:51.777 --> 00:06:53.245
-Yeah, I mean,
I'm an introvert

00:06:53.245 --> 00:06:56.081
and I feel like
I've always lived very much

00:06:56.081 --> 00:06:58.484
from the inside out,
and I will say

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:01.386
I mostly interviewed
introverts for the podcast.

00:07:01.386 --> 00:07:04.456
And then you were kind of
the surprise guest at the end

00:07:04.456 --> 00:07:05.891
where it was like,
surprise,

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:09.194
you're going to interview
an extrovert to learn some tips.

00:07:09.194 --> 00:07:12.364
And guess what.
It's a professional interviewer.

00:07:12.364 --> 00:07:13.633
Good luck!

00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:15.468
[ Laughter ]

00:07:15.468 --> 00:07:17.402
So yeah, it was
pretty much my nightmare.

00:07:17.402 --> 00:07:19.204
-Yeah, well, I had a great time.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:19.204 --> 00:07:22.507
I want you to know it was not
my nightmare.

00:07:22.507 --> 00:07:25.177
The book is wonderful.
The podcast is also wonderful.

00:07:25.177 --> 00:07:26.311
And thank you so much
for being back.

00:07:26.311 --> 00:07:27.613
It is a joy to have you.

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:29.582
-Thank you for having me.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:31.450
-Aparna Nancherla, everybody.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:34.353
"Unreliable Narrator"
is on sale now.

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:36.222
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

